{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.BorelWatson","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.BorelWatson","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.StirlingUpperBound"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Borel.gevrey1_remainder_expDecay","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Borel.gevrey1_remainder_expDecay","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BorelWatson.lean#L65-L117","doc":"**Gevrey-1 remainder ⇒ exponential decay (archimedean).** If a nonnegative\nreal `a` is dominated by the Gevrey-1 truncation bound `a ≤ C·N!·xᴺ` for\n*every* `N`, then `a` is exponentially small:\n`a ≤ C·e²·x^{-1/2}·e^{−1/x}` for `0 < x ≤ 1`. Evaluating the bound at the\nleast-term index `N = ⌊1/x⌋` and applying brick 1\n(`factorial_le_stirling_upper`, `N! ≤ e√N(N/e)ᴺ`) collapses `(Nx/e)ᴺ` to\n`e^{−N} ≤ e·e^{−1/x}`. This is the analytic heart of Watson uniqueness —\nexponential (super-polynomial) smallness that a >π-sector\nPhragmén–Lindelöf argument (brick 3) turns into `a = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Borel.factorialPow_succ_le_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Borel.factorialPow_succ_le_iff","kind":"theorem","line":39,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BorelWatson.lean#L39-L61","doc":"**Least-term index (pre-archimedean).** For `0 < x` in any linearly\nordered field, the truncation term `t_N = N!·xᴺ` stops decreasing exactly\nwhen `(N+1)·x > 1`: `t_{N+1} ≤ t_N ↔ (N+1)·x ≤ 1`. No `exp`, no\ncompleteness — pure order theory; the least term therefore sits at\n`N ≈ 1/x`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}